<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-num-project-conclusion">
  <title>Conclusion</title>

  <p>
    End the report with a coherent conclusion that brings together the
    important findings from the entire numerical-data project. Your
    conclusion should be <alert>no more than one page</alert>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-conclusion-content">
    <title>What to Include</title>
    <p>
      Your conclusion should:
      <ol marker="1)">
        <li><p>return to all three research questions and answer them
        using the analyses you performed,</p></li>
        <li><p>summarize the main findings of the one-mean analysis,
        the paired or independent two-mean test, and the one-way ANOVA,
        including Tukey findings when applicable,</p></li>
        <li><p>explain the biological, health, or scientific meaning
        of the findings, including the sizes of observed differences
        when relevant,</p></li>
        <li><p>discuss the most important limitations of your data
        and analyses, and</p></li>
        <li><p>explain what conclusions are supported and how broadly
        they can reasonably be applied.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="num-project-conclusion-writing">
    <title>Writing the Conclusion</title>
    <p>
      Write a connected discussion that emphasizes what you learned from
      the data. Include important numerical findings when they clarify
      the message. Keep the conclusion consistent with the results,
      including findings that were not statistically significant.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>
</subsection>
